---
title: "How Decoy Works: Where It Fits in Your Data Pipeline"
description: "How Decoy scans, subsets, masks, generates, validates, and documents a dataset, where it sits in an existing pipeline, how the CLI and Platform share one engine, and what the measured performance looks like."
bodyClass: page-how
motion: true
---
<section class="hero hero--page">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">How it works</p>
      <h1 data-split>One step in a pipeline you already run</h1>
      <p class="lede">Decoy does not replace your warehouse, your ETL, or your CI. It sits between a source you trust and the environments that should never see raw PII, and it leaves evidence of what it did.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="pipeline">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="pipeline-stage" data-pipeline>
    <div class="flow" data-flow role="list">
      <div class="flow-step flow-step--edge" role="listitem" data-stage="source"><strong>Source</strong><span>Files, object storage, or a Postgres snapshot</span></div>
      <div class="flow-step" role="listitem" data-stage="scan"><strong>Scan</strong><span>Profiles columns and suggests strategies</span></div>
      <div class="flow-step" role="listitem" data-stage="subset"><strong>Subset</strong><span>Follow foreign keys to a small, intact slice</span></div>
      <div class="flow-step" role="listitem" data-stage="mask"><strong>Mask or generate</strong><span>Keyed, deterministic transforms per column</span></div>
      <div class="flow-step" role="listitem" data-stage="validate"><strong>Validate</strong><span>Residual PII, FK parity, distribution integrity</span></div>
      <div class="flow-step flow-step--safe" role="listitem" data-stage="evidence"><strong>Evidence</strong><span>Manifest, report, audit entry</span></div>
      <div class="flow-step flow-step--edge" role="listitem" data-stage="target"><strong>Target</strong><span>Local, S3, GCS, dev and test databases</span></div>
    </div>

    <div class="stage-cards" data-reveal>
      <article class="card stage-card" data-stage="source">
        <div class="stage-copy">
          <div class="stage-heading">
            <div class="icon-badge">{% icon "database" %}</div>
            <div><span class="stage-number">01 / 07</span><h3>Bring the data to Decoy</h3></div>
          </div>
          <p>Choose a local CSV or Parquet file, an object in S3 or GCS, or a read-only PostgreSQL snapshot in the Platform. Decoy reads inside your environment; there is no hosted Decoy control plane in the data path.</p>
          <dl class="stage-details">
            <div><dt>What happens</dt><dd>The source is resolved and checked before the transformation starts.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Best fit</dt><dd>Teams that already export production data to files or object storage for lower environments.</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </div>
        <div class="stage-visual source-map" role="img" aria-label="Local files, object storage, and a PostgreSQL snapshot feeding a Decoy run inside the customer's environment">
          <div class="source-list">
            <span>{% icon "file" %}<b>Local files</b><small>CSV · Parquet</small></span>
            <span>{% icon "cloud" %}<b>Object storage</b><small>S3 · GCS</small></span>
            <span>{% icon "database" %}<b>PostgreSQL</b><small>read-only snapshot</small></span>
          </div>
          <div class="stage-arrow" aria-hidden="true"><span></span></div>
          <div class="decoy-node"><img src="/images/decoy/mark.png" alt="" width="42" height="42"><strong>Decoy</strong><small>your infrastructure</small></div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="card stage-card" data-stage="scan">
        <div class="stage-copy">
          <div class="stage-heading">
            <div class="icon-badge">{% icon "scan" %}</div>
            <div><span class="stage-number">02 / 07</span><h3>Find the columns that need a decision</h3></div>
          </div>
          <p>The scan profiles every column, runs 25 value detectors, groups findings by domain, and proposes a draft strategy. It makes the first pass faster without silently deciding policy for you.</p>
          <dl class="stage-details">
            <div><dt>What happens</dt><dd>Likely identity, contact, financial, health, and location data is surfaced for review.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Best fit</dt><dd>Unknown datasets, inherited schemas, and a first masking policy that needs human review.</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </div>
        <div class="stage-visual scan-result" aria-label="Example scan findings for a customer table">
          <div class="mini-toolbar"><span>customers.csv</span><span class="status-quiet">25 detectors</span></div>
          <div class="scan-row scan-row--head"><span>column</span><span>finding</span><span>suggestion</span></div>
          <div class="scan-row"><code>email</code><span><i class="risk-dot"></i>contact</span><strong>faker</strong></div>
          <div class="scan-row"><code>ssn</code><span><i class="risk-dot"></i>identity</span><strong>fpe</strong></div>
          <div class="scan-row"><code>dob</code><span><i class="risk-dot"></i>identity</span><strong>date_shift</strong></div>
          <div class="scan-review">3 suggestions · review required</div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="card stage-card" data-stage="subset">
        <div class="stage-copy">
          <div class="stage-heading">
            <div class="icon-badge">{% icon "link" %}</div>
            <div><span class="stage-number">03 / 07</span><h3>Cut the volume, keep the relationships</h3></div>
          </div>
          <p>Start with a useful slice, then follow declared foreign keys until every retained child still points to a retained parent. A fan-out budget keeps the subset bounded.</p>
          <dl class="stage-details">
            <div><dt>What happens</dt><dd>Related tables close to a referentially complete subset instead of becoming disconnected samples.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Best fit</dt><dd>Fast local databases, compact integration fixtures, and representative CI datasets.</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </div>
        <div class="stage-visual relation-map" role="img" aria-label="A subset retaining 1,000 customers, 5,000 orders, and 12,400 order items with no orphaned foreign keys">
          <div class="relation-node relation-node--root"><span>customers</span><strong>1,000</strong><small>seed rows</small></div>
          <div class="relation-line relation-line--one" aria-hidden="true"></div>
          <div class="relation-node"><span>orders</span><strong>5,000</strong><small>customer_id intact</small></div>
          <div class="relation-line relation-line--two" aria-hidden="true"></div>
          <div class="relation-node"><span>order_items</span><strong>12,400</strong><small>order_id intact</small></div>
          <div class="relation-proof">{% icon "check" %} 0 orphaned rows</div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="card stage-card" data-stage="mask">
        <div class="stage-copy">
          <div class="stage-heading">
            <div class="icon-badge">{% icon "key" %}</div>
            <div><span class="stage-number">04 / 07</span><h3>Transform each column deliberately</h3></div>
          </div>
          <p>Assign one of 23 masking strategies or generate new rows from 12 generation types. Keyed strategies use your secret so the same source value maps to the same safe value across tables and runs.</p>
          <dl class="stage-details">
            <div><dt>What happens</dt><dd>Identifiers, names, dates, free text, codes, and categories each get a strategy suited to their role.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Best fit</dt><dd>Staging and test data that must stay realistic enough for joins, workflows, and regressions.</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </div>
        <div class="stage-visual mask-preview">
          <div class="mini-toolbar"><span>customers.parquet</span><span class="status-quiet">before → after</span></div>
          <div class="mask-preview-grid mask-preview-grid--head"><span>column</span><span>source</span><span>safe output</span></div>
          <div class="mask-preview-grid"><code>email</code><span class="raw">maria@acme.com</span><span class="safe">lena@example.net</span></div>
          <div class="mask-preview-grid"><code>ssn</code><span class="raw">612-44-9031</span><span class="safe">927-10-5583</span></div>
          <div class="mask-preview-grid"><code>dob</code><span class="raw">1987-03-12</span><span class="safe">1987-02-27</span></div>
          <div class="strategy-strip"><span>faker</span><span>fpe</span><span>date_shift</span></div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="card stage-card" data-stage="validate">
        <div class="stage-copy">
          <div class="stage-heading">
            <div class="icon-badge">{% icon "shield" %}</div>
            <div><span class="stage-number">05 / 07</span><h3>Fail before unsafe data moves on</h3></div>
          </div>
          <p>Validation checks the pipeline before a run. The integrity report then looks for residual PII, foreign-key drift, and distribution changes in the output. Findings can stop CI with exit code 4.</p>
          <dl class="stage-details">
            <div><dt>What happens</dt><dd>Configuration, output safety, relational integrity, and fidelity are checked at explicit gates.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Best fit</dt><dd>Automated refresh jobs where unsafe or structurally broken output must never publish quietly.</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </div>
        <div class="stage-visual check-report" aria-label="Validation report with configuration, residual PII, foreign keys, and distribution checks passing">
          <div class="mini-toolbar"><span>integrity report</span><span class="status-pass">exit 0</span></div>
          <div class="check-row"><span>{% icon "check" %} config</span><strong>valid</strong></div>
          <div class="check-row"><span>{% icon "check" %} residual PII</span><strong>0 findings</strong></div>
          <div class="check-row"><span>{% icon "check" %} foreign keys</span><strong>parity held</strong></div>
          <div class="check-row"><span>{% icon "check" %} distributions</span><strong>within policy</strong></div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="card stage-card" data-stage="evidence">
        <div class="stage-copy">
          <div class="stage-heading">
            <div class="icon-badge">{% icon "file" %}</div>
            <div><span class="stage-number">06 / 07</span><h3>Leave a record that can be checked later</h3></div>
          </div>
          <p>The CLI can write an evidence manifest with the pipeline hash, file fingerprints, and row counts without storing raw values. The Platform also records actions in an append-only, hash-chained audit log.</p>
          <dl class="stage-details">
            <div><dt>What happens</dt><dd>The run produces an artifact for verification; shared Platform activity gets an accountable history.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Best fit</dt><dd>Change review, audit preparation, incident investigation, and proving which pipeline produced a dataset.</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </div>
        <div class="stage-visual evidence-preview" aria-label="Evidence manifest linked to a hash-chained Platform audit entry">
          <div class="manifest-sheet">
            <span><b>pipeline_fingerprint</b><code>sha256:8f3a…91d2</code></span>
            <span><b>row_counts</b><code>customers · 1M → 1M</code></span>
            <span><b>output_fingerprints</b><code>customers · sha256:42b7…</code></span>
            <span><b>manifest_hash</b><code>sha256:50ec…</code></span>
          </div>
          <div class="evidence-link" aria-hidden="true"></div>
          <div class="audit-stamp"><span>audit #4817</span><strong>job.run · ok</strong><small>prev 9c1e…b2 → e07a…41</small></div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="card stage-card" data-stage="target">
        <div class="stage-copy">
          <div class="stage-heading">
            <div class="icon-badge">{% icon "box" %}</div>
            <div><span class="stage-number">07 / 07</span><h3>Publish safe data to the systems that need it</h3></div>
          </div>
          <p>Write CSV or Parquet locally or to supported object storage, then let your existing delivery process load the result into development, test, CI, or analytics environments. Decoy does not receive the source or output.</p>
          <dl class="stage-details">
            <div><dt>What happens</dt><dd>The validated output lands at an explicit destination after the run and its evidence are complete.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Best fit</dt><dd>Repeatable lower-environment refreshes without replacing your warehouse, ETL, or scheduler.</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </div>
        <div class="stage-visual target-map" role="img" aria-label="Validated Decoy output delivered as local files or object storage for development, testing, CI, and analytics">
          <div class="target-origin"><span>{% icon "check" %}</span><strong>validated output</strong><small>CSV · Parquet</small></div>
          <div class="target-lines" aria-hidden="true"></div>
          <div class="target-grid">
            <span><b>Local</b><small>files</small></span>
            <span><b>S3 / GCS</b><small>object storage</small></span>
            <span><b>Dev + test</b><small>refresh jobs</small></span>
            <span><b>CI + analytics</b><small>safe fixtures</small></span>
          </div>
          <p>{% icon "shield" %} Nothing is sent to Decoy</p>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="inside-a-run">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
      <p class="eyebrow">Inside a run</p>
      <h2>What happens between <code>decoy run</code> and exit 0</h2>
    </div>
    <ol class="grid grid-2" data-reveal style="list-style: none; padding: 0; counter-reset: run">
      <li class="card"><h4>1. Validate and plan</h4><p>The YAML is checked against the schema without touching data. A plan is compiled and stamped with the seed-protocol version so the run is reproducible.</p></li>
      <li class="card"><h4>2. Derive keys</h4><p>The mask secret is loaded from an environment variable or file. Per-strategy keys derive from it with HKDF-SHA256; nothing is stored.</p></li>
      <li class="card"><h4>3. Transform</h4><p>Tables stream through pandas with a compiled Rust kernel for hashing and other hot strategies. Large foreign-key work takes an out-of-core route through DuckDB.</p></li>
      <li class="card"><h4>4. Validate and write evidence</h4><p>Outputs are written, the integrity check runs, and the manifest and report are emitted. Exit codes tell CI exactly what happened.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="surfaces">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
      <p class="eyebrow">Surfaces</p>
      <h2>Three ways to run the same engine</h2>
    </div>
    <div role="tablist" class="tabs" aria-label="Ways to use Decoy">
      <button role="tab" class="tab" id="tab-cli" aria-controls="panel-cli" aria-selected="true" data-hash="cli">CLI</button>
      <button role="tab" class="tab" id="tab-platform" aria-controls="panel-platform" aria-selected="false" data-hash="platform">Platform</button>
      <button role="tab" class="tab" id="tab-engine" aria-controls="panel-engine" aria-selected="false" data-hash="engine">Engine</button>
    </div>
    <div role="tabpanel" class="tab-panel" id="panel-cli" aria-labelledby="tab-cli">
      <div class="grid grid-2">
        <div>
          <h3>For the developer and the build</h3>
          <p class="muted">Runs locally and in CI with no server or account. Validate a config, run it, gate on the exit code, keep the evidence artifact. Free and source-available.</p>
          <a class="link-arrow" href="/cli/">The CLI page {% icon "arrow-right" %}</a>
        </div>
        <div class="terminal"><div class="terminal-bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>ci.yml</span></div><pre>{% snippet "ci.yml" %}</pre></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div role="tabpanel" class="tab-panel" id="panel-platform" aria-labelledby="tab-platform" hidden>
      <div class="grid grid-2">
        <div>
          <h3>For the team and the reviewer</h3>
          <p class="muted">A self-hosted web app on the same engine: a visual builder, shared sources and job history, roles and single sign-on, and a hash-chained audit log. ${{ site.pricing.platformMonthly }} a month per instance.</p>
          <a class="link-arrow" href="/platform/">The Platform page {% icon "arrow-right" %}</a>
        </div>
        <div class="card"><h4>What the Platform adds</h4><ul class="muted small"><li>Job Studio builder and run history</li><li>S3, GCS, and Postgres sources</li><li>Four roles, OIDC SSO, API keys</li><li>Tamper-evident audit log with export</li><li>Alerts by email, Slack, webhook</li></ul></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div role="tabpanel" class="tab-panel" id="panel-engine" aria-labelledby="tab-engine" hidden>
      <div class="grid grid-2">
        <div>
          <h3>For your own code</h3>
          <p class="muted">The Apache-2.0 Python engine accepts Arrow, pandas, and Polars tables in memory and returns the same deterministic results the CLI produces. Both products import it; neither calls the other.</p>
          <a class="link-arrow" href="/docs/reference/configuration">Configuration reference {% icon "arrow-right" %}</a>
        </div>
        <div class="card"><h4>Data plane</h4><p>pandas for masking with a Rust kernel for hot strategies, pyarrow between stages, Polars for subsetting, DuckDB for out-of-core joins. Faker for providers, OpenDP for differential privacy.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="performance">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
      <p class="eyebrow">Performance</p>
      <h2>How fast, and will it fit on your box</h2>
      <p>Speed depends on the operation, the row count, the strategy mix (hash is cheap; FPE and free text are not), and the hardware. This matrix reports each core operation at 1M, 10M, and 100M rows: sustained throughput, end-to-end wall time, and peak memory. Every number is measured on the default <code>decoy run</code> path, so until the benchmarks land the cells read Not measured rather than an estimate.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption class="small muted" style="text-align: left; caption-side: bottom; padding: var(--space-3) 1rem 0">
          {% if performance.measured_on %}Measured on {{ performance.measured_on }}, {{ performance.hardware }}, engine {{ performance.engine_version }}.{% else %}Benchmarks pending. Every cell fills from the default <code>decoy run</code> path with its date, hardware, engine version, and route.{% endif %} Throughput is end-to-end rows per second; wall is the full command time; peak memory is the maximum observed. 100M is a linear projection from the measured sizes, labeled where shown.
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Operation</th>
            {% for scale in performance.scales %}<th scope="col">{{ scale }}</th>{% endfor %}
            <th scope="col">Peak memory</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {% for op in performance.operations %}
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">
              {{ op.operation }}
              <span class="small muted" style="display: block; font-weight: 400; text-transform: none; letter-spacing: 0">{{ op.detail }}</span>
              {% if op.route %}<span class="small muted" style="display: block; font-weight: 400; text-transform: none; letter-spacing: 0">Route: {{ op.route }}</span>{% endif %}
            </th>
            {% for cell in op.cells %}
            <td>
              {% if cell.throughput or cell.wall %}
                {% if cell.throughput %}<strong>{{ cell.throughput }}</strong>{% endif %}
                {% if cell.wall %}<span class="small muted" style="display: block">{{ cell.wall }}</span>{% endif %}
                {% if cell.projected %}<span class="small muted" style="display: block">projected (linear)</span>{% endif %}
              {% else %}
                <span class="small muted">Not measured</span>
              {% endif %}
            </td>
            {% endfor %}
            <td>{% if op.peak_ram %}{{ op.peak_ram }}{% else %}<span class="small muted">Not measured</span>{% endif %}</td>
          </tr>
          {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="small muted" style="margin-top: var(--space-4)">The numbers are measured on the default run path (the route a normal <code>decoy run</code> takes), on Linux x86-64. No performance evidence is published yet for Windows, macOS, or ARM64.</p>
  </div>
</section>

